    text: "Emotions are the feelings that colour every human experience, and English has an exceptionally rich vocabulary for describing them. The basic emotions — happiness, sadness, anger, fear, surprise, and disgust — are universally recognised across cultures. Happiness ranges from mild contentment to overwhelming joy and ecstasy. We feel glad when something good happens, cheerful when our mood is bright, and delighted when pleasantly surprised. Sadness encompasses grief, sorrow, disappointment, and melancholy. We feel upset when hurt, miserable when deeply unhappy, and heartbroken after a significant loss. Anger varies from mild annoyance and irritation to furious rage. We become frustrated when blocked from a goal, and outraged by injustice. Fear spans nervousness and anxiety to terror and panic. We feel worried about uncertain outcomes, scared of specific threats, and terrified of extreme danger. Surprise can be positive (amazed, astonished, thrilled) or negative (shocked, stunned, horrified). Disgust is our reaction to something revolting or morally offensive. Beyond these basics, English recognises complex emotional states: boredom, confusion, embarrassment, guilt, shame, pride, relief, hope, and gratitude. Understanding and expressing emotions precisely is essential for relationships, mental health discussions, literature, and the kind of nuanced communication that marks advanced English proficiency.",
